==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|mysse layen}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|mis|lay}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mɪsˈleɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-mislay.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|mislays|mislaying|mislaid}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# To [[leave]] or [[lay]] something in the wrong place and then [[forget]] where one put it.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971 | title=Tokugawa to Meiji Japan: a Resource Guide for Teachers |author=Nancy Anne Dyer | page=140| passage=He cannot see well, '''mislays''' his glasses, and frequently mismends the nets.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[err]] in [[place|placing]] something.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Barlow (bishop of Lincoln)|William Barlow]]|title=A Sermon Preached at Paules Crosse, on the First Sunday in Lent;{{nb...|Martij 1. 1600. With a Short Discourse of the Late Earle of Essex His Confession, and Penitence, before and at the Time of His Death. By William Barlow Doctor of Diuinitie. Whereunto Is Annexed a True Copie, in Substance, of the Behauiour, Speache, and Prayer of the Said Earle at the Time of His Execution.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} {{quote-gloss|R. Read}} for Mathew Law,{{nb...|dwelling in Paules Church-yard neere Watling-streete.}}|year=1601|section=signature C iii., recto|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_a-sermon-preached-at-pau_barlow-william_1601/page/n36/mode/1up|passage=[F]ire, if it be well and rightly vſed, burneth in the houſe to the good and pꝛofit of the familie: but if '''miſlayed''' oꝛ abuſed, burneth the houſe to the vndoing of the inhabitantes: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Sylvester Du Bartas|day=1|page=12|passage=You haue miſ-caſt in your ''Arithmetike'', / '''Miſ-laid''' your Counters, groapingly yee ſeek / In nights blacke darknes for the ſecret things / Seal’d in the Casket of the King of Kings.}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Works|volume=IV|chapter=Sermon CX. Preached to the King, at the Court. The Second Sermon on Genesis i. 26.|page=532|passage=First, ''Scio cui credidi'', I know whom I have believed in: I have not '''mislaid''' my foundation; my foundation is Christ; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Crowne|J{{quote-gloss|ohn}} Crown]]|chapter=The Third Act|title=[[w:Juliana (play)|Juliana or The Princess of Poland. A Tragicomedy.{{nb...|As It Is Acted at His Royal Highness the Duke of York’s Theatre.}}]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Will. Cademan {{...|at the Popes-Head in the lower Walk in the New-Exchange,}} and Will. Birch{{nb...|at the lower end of Cheapside, 1671.}}|year=1671|page=35|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_juliana-or-the-princess_crowne-john_1671/page/(35)/mode/1up|passage=Oh heavens! her grief '''miſlayes''' her noble reaſon, what ſhall we do}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Conduct|section=4|pages=19–20|pageref=19|passage=This being ſo that Defects and Weakneſs in Mens Underſtandings, as well as other Faculties, come from want of a right uſe of their own Minds, I am apt to think the fault is generally '''miſlaid''' upon Nature, and there is often a Complaint of want of Parts when the fault lies in want of a due improvement of them.}}

